  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n07Z water vapor had the closed upper low moving across northern AZ \nthis morning with a plum of tropical moisture feeding into the \ncentral plains. At the surface the cold front had push a little \nfurther south to near Baldwin City and Lebo. Much of the convection \nhad shifted south over the Ozarks with some stratiform rain lingering\nover the eastern counties. \n\nModels indicate there may still be some isentropic lift occurring \nover the boundary across central KS and think this is the cause of \nthe scattered shower activity from Minneapolis to near Emporia. So \nhave some 30 to 60 percent chance POPs for areas along and south of \nI-70. The upper low is expected to begin coming out into the high \nplains this evening with a lead shortwave lifting across the area. \nIn response to this models show low level flow backing to the south \nagain with increasing low level moisture advection commencing. The \nsurface front is progged to still be hung up across east central KS \nand most guidance brings a weak surface wave through eastern KS by \nThursday morning. This boundary could focus another round of heavy \nrain as precipital water values still remain close to record levels. \nSo there is some concern that parts of east central KS could get \nanother 1 to 2 inches late Wednesday night and Thursday morning with \nthe surface wave. Will extend the southern tier of counties through \nThursday morning while letting the rest of the watch go with this \nforecast package. Thanks for the collaboration EAX, ICT and SGF. \n\nClouds and northeast surface winds are expected to limit much of a \ndiurnal change in temps today or tonight. In fact parts of east \ncentral KS could see temps slowly rising before dawn Thursday as the \nlow level warm air and moisture advection ramps up. Then a stronger \nnorthern stream upper trough is progged to dive south and absorb the \nclosed low Thursday night. This northern stream wave will also \nbring a stronger push of dry air into the area with the front moving \ninto MO by Thursday evening. This should bring an end to precip \nchances and also much cooler temperatures for the end of the week. \nSaturday morning still looks like a good radiational cooling setup \nwith the center of the surface ridge passing through the area. So \nhave kept a mention of frost with lows in the middle 30s. It looks \nlike at some point we will need a frost advisory. The 01Z NBM has \nthe probability of lows Saturday morning reaching the freezing mark \nbetween 20 and 50 percent. So a freeze warning is not out of the \nquestion. \n\nBy Sunday the surface ridge is forecast to weaken and move to the \neast of the forecast area. This should promote a return to southerly \nlow level flow and cause temps to trend warmer. Although it looks \nlike highs early next week should remain in the 70s. Operational \nsolutions still indicate a weak shortwave potentially moving through \nthe central plains on Sunday and Sunday night. The GFS is the only \nsolution with QPF and more than 80 percent of the ensembles from the \nGFS/ECMWF/Canadian favor a dry forecast. This is in line with the \nlatest NBM which keeps chances around 10 percent.",
